# Markdown Style — AI-Engineer Curriculum

This is a curriculum repo of dense architectural notes. Use the existing files in `7. AI System Architecture/` as the style reference.

## Heading levels

- Line 1 of any numbered note is `# X.Y. Title Case`. Match the filename exactly.
- Top-level sections use `## N. Section Title` (numbered).
- Subsections use `### N.M Subtitle`.
- Never skip heading levels.

## Body

- Prefer **markdown pipe tables** when comparing 3+ items along the same axes. Prose comparisons are a smell.
- Use **code fences with an explicit language** (` ```text` for ASCII diagrams is fine; never use unlabeled triple-backticks).
- Use **opinionated callouts** as inline blockquotes: `> Why it matters:`, `> Architectural takeaway:`, `> Lesson:`. They mark the takeaway in a sea of description.
- Use **em-dash `—` (U+2014)**, not double hyphens, when joining clauses or in titles.
- Cross-link with **relative paths and URL-encoded spaces**: `[7.1. Claude Platform Managed Agent](./7.1.%20Claude%20Platform%20Managed%20Agent.md)`.

## Voice

- Direct. State the claim, then justify.
- Architectural. Talk about primitives, boundaries, tradeoffs — not features.
- Vendor-neutral by default. Vendor specifics live in tables or call-outs.
- No marketing words: "powerful", "seamless", "robust", "industry-leading", "cutting-edge".

## Length

- Substantive notes target **150–250 lines**. If a note grows beyond ~300, split it.
- One-liner stubs (e.g., `prompt-caching.md`) are placeholders awaiting promotion to numbered notes.

## Forbidden

- Emoji decoration (the 🟢🟡🔴 build-tier convention is the only allowed exception, and only when used semantically as in `7.2`).
- Fabricated references. Every link in a `## References` section must be a URL you actually visited or one the user provided.
- Editing files outside the curriculum tree without an explicit instruction.
